# Specification Decisions

Decisions made during creation of `loop/specification.md`. Where the developer was
asked and did not respond within the interactive window, the recommended option was
chosen and is recorded here. Any of these can be overridden by the developer; if one
is overridden, update `loop/specification.md` to match and note the change here.

## D1. Game scope: "faithful core" (chosen; user asked 2026-07-02, no response — recommended option taken)

- **Options:** (a) Faithful core, (b) Minimal MVP, (c) Full arcade clone.
- **Chosen:** (a) Faithful core — single-player game with all 5 classic enemy types
  (Flipper, Tanker, Spiker, Fuseball, Pulsar), 16 well geometries cycling with color
  changes, Superzapper, lives, arcade-style scoring, endless level progression.
- **Why:** The description says "inspired by" Tempest, not a clone. Faithful core
  captures what makes Tempest recognizable while avoiding ROM-archaeology (exact
  scoring tables, 2-player alternating, cabinet quirks) that adds effort without
  serving the stated goal.

## D2. Platform: desktop-first, keyboard + mouse (chosen; user asked 2026-07-02, no response — recommended option taken)

- **Options:** (a) Desktop-first keyboard + mouse, (b) Desktop + mobile touch, (c) Keyboard only.
- **Chosen:** (a) Desktop-first. Keyboard is the primary control; mouse is supported
  as an alternative because it maps naturally to the original's spinner knob.
  Touch/mobile is out of scope for v1 (listed as a future enhancement).
- **Why:** Touch support roughly doubles input/UI design and testing surface for a
  game whose core feel is precise rotary movement.

## D3. Tech stack: TypeScript + Vite + Canvas 2D, zero runtime deps (chosen; user asked 2026-07-02, no response — recommended option taken)

- **Options:** (a) TypeScript + Vite + Canvas 2D, (b) plain JS with no build step,
  (c) TypeScript + WebGL (three.js/regl).
- **Chosen:** (a). Vitest for unit tests, `tsc --strict` for type checking, zero
  runtime dependencies, static `dist/` output.
- **Why:** Tempest is line art; Canvas 2D with a manual 3D→2D projection reproduces
  the vector look without a WebGL pipeline or a runtime dependency. TypeScript fits
  the developer's stated preference for typed code.

## D4. Visual style: neon vector/wireframe (chosen without asking — definitional)

Glowing stroked outlines on black, per-16-level color cycling. A Tempest-inspired
game that doesn't look like vector graphics fails the "inspired by" test.

## D5. Audio: Web Audio API synthesized SFX, no audio assets (chosen without asking)

Sound effects are synthesized at runtime (oscillators/noise). No music (the original
had none). Mute toggle persisted in settings. Avoids asset licensing and keeps the
build dependency-free.

## D6. Persistence: localStorage (chosen without asking)

High-score table (top 10, 3-initial entry), settings (mute), and highest level
reached are stored in `localStorage`. No backend, no network calls.

## D7. Single-player only (chosen without asking)

The original's 2-player mode is alternating (not simultaneous) — it is a coin-op
artifact, not core to the game. Out of scope.

## D8. Tankers carry Flippers only in v1 (chosen without asking)

The original introduces Fuseball-tankers and Pulsar-tankers in very late levels.
V1 tankers always release two Flippers. Variant cargo is a listed future enhancement.

## D9. Scoring is "arcade-style", not ROM-accurate (chosen without asking)

Point values are inspired by the original but defined by this spec (see the scoring
table in `loop/specification.md`). Superzapper kills award no points, to keep the
panic button from being the optimal scoring strategy.

## D10. Starting-level selection is included, simplified (chosen without asking)

The original's "SKILL STEP" starting-level choice is a signature feature and a big
part of replayability, so it stays: the player may start at any level they have
previously reached, capped at level 9 for a fresh profile's first game. No score
bonus for starting deep (the original awarded one; dropped for simplicity).

## D11. No attract-mode gameplay demo (chosen without asking)

The title screen shows the logo, high scores, and controls, but does not run an
AI-played demo game. A demo player is real work with no gameplay payoff.

## D12. Planning docs live in `loop/`, game code at repo root (chosen without asking)

`loop/` holds description, spec, guides, checklists, and decision/review logs.
The game itself (package.json, index.html, src/, tests/) lives at the repository
root so the repo is the game project.

## D13. Mouse control is pointer-lock-only (review round 1; refines D2)

Review found the original "mouse X maps to rim position, relative movement
without pointer lock" scheme unimplementable on closed wells (cursor clamps at
screen edges, killing continuous rotation) and riddled with Escape-key/pointer-
lock conflicts. Decision: mouse control requires the Pointer Lock API (click to
engage); keyboard is the always-available fallback; lock loss auto-pauses via
`pointerlockchange`, and resume-by-click re-requests the lock. See spec §5.

## D14. Destroying enemy shots scores 0 points (review round 1)

Any positive value makes farming the last enemy's shots (especially a Pulsar,
which never leaves the well) a dominant, unbounded high-score strategy. Zero
matches the original closely enough and kills the exploit. Spike trims stay at
1 point: rate-bounded (under ~6 pts/s at the 0.18 s fireInterval), requires standing
in harm's way, and the wave still can't end until the Spiker is dead — not a
viable farm.

## D15. "Fuseball" name restored; lane-based movement kept (review round 1)

The enemy this spec previously called "Fuzzball" is the original's **Fuseball**;
renamed for fidelity. The original Fuseball rides lane *edges*, which would add a
second positioning system for one enemy; v1 keeps lane-based movement with
erratic speed to preserve the feel. Edge-riding is listed as a non-goal (§14.7).

## D16. Death on a spike during warp does not replay the descent (review round 1)

The original makes you re-run the descent after a spike death. Rejected for v1:
replaying punishes one mistake twice and adds a state-machine special case. The
level counts as complete (bonus already awarded at wave completion), a life is
lost, and play continues at the next level. Recorded as an inspired-by deviation
(spec §9, non-goal §14.1).

## D17. Determinism is scoped to a single JS engine (review round 1)

The replay/determinism guarantee is bit-exact reproduction given the same seed
and input-snapshot sequence **on the same JS engine/build**. Cross-browser
floating-point identity is explicitly not claimed (transcendental functions are
implementation-defined across engines).

## D18. Every well has exactly 16 lanes (review round 1)

Matches the original's 16-sector webs, removes the spec's earlier "≈14–16,
≥12" wobble, and makes lane math, difficulty tuning, and geometry authoring
uniform (closed = 16 rim vertices, open = 17).

## D19. PAUSED is an app-layer overlay, not a sim state (review round 2)

Options: (a) app-layer pause (stop calling tick(); sim frozen), or (b) add
pause signals to the input snapshot and keep PAUSED in the sim state machine.
Chose (a): it keeps the determinism/replay contract clean (replays are
pause-agnostic), matches "auto-pause stops the accumulator", and moves
browser-event plumbing (blur/visibility/pointer-lock) out of the pure sim.
The §13 state-machine test covers the sim-owned diagram only.

## D20. Level-clear bonus capped at the level-96 anchor (review round 2)

With difficulty flat past level 96 but the 100 × level bonus unbounded,
bonus-life income eventually exceeds one life per wave and endgame runs would
end by fatigue, not skill. Bonus = 100 × min(level, 96).

## D21. Fuseball scoring is depth-banded 250/500/750 (review round 2)

The original's proximity risk/reward on its scariest enemy is a gameplay
structure, not point-table trivia; a flat 250 makes "kill it ASAP at long
range" strictly optimal. Bands: kill at depth > 2/3 → 250, 1/3–2/3 → 500,
< 1/3 (near the rim) → 750.

## D22. Text is canvas-drawn stroke lettering; no font files (review round 2)

A bundled font is filled glyphs (conflicting with the all-line-art visual
identity), needs async load handling, and adds a licensing check. A
Hershey/Vectrex-style stroke-segment data module keeps everything line art and
zero-asset.

## D23. Superzapper on an empty well consumes the use (review round 2)

Simplest deterministic rule; punishes panic misuse mildly; avoids "is the well
empty *this tick*" edge-case disputes in tests. Also: Superzapper input is
accepted only during PLAYING.

## D24. Rim contact is symmetric; rim flips run at 0.5 × FlipInt (review round 2)

As previously written, a player could stand on a resting rim Flipper's lane
unharmed and kill it point-blank risk-free, inverting the rim chase's tension
(and contradicting Fuseball contact semantics). Now: sharing a lane with any
rim-resident lethal enemy kills, whoever moved; the mid-flip save survives via
the flip-occupancy rule. Rim flips are faster than mid-well flips so the chase
stays threatening.

## D25. Fuseball rim residency is temporary (review round 2)

A permanent rim Fuseball was effectively unkillable (shooting it requires
occupying the lane that kills you), stalled wave completion indefinitely, and
cornered players on open wells. It now crawls the rim for fuseballRimTime,
then descends and resumes climbing — original-like wandering, and the §6 tick
order (shots resolve before contact) makes the in-lane kill fair.

## D26. Deterministic spawn and fire schedulers (review round 2)

FireInt as a bare "mean" and unspecified spawn type/lane selection were
untestable and non-deterministic. Now: per-enemy next-shot delay uniform in
[0.5, 1.5] × FireInt (suppressed attempts redraw); spawn type drawn weighted by
remaining budget, lane uniform — all via the sim RNG.

## D27. Pulses are lethal for their full duration; no flips during telegraph (review round 2)

Instant-only lethality let players walk through visibly electrified lanes, and
a Pulsar flipping mid-telegraph could kill with almost no warning (late-game:
7 Pulsars × 3 lanes ≥ 16 lanes = unavoidable deaths). Flips freeze from
telegraph start through pulse end; the electrified lane kills for the whole
pulseDuration, including players entering mid-pulse.

## D28. Mid-well flips seek the player (review round 2)

Flip direction during the climb was unspecified. Chose player-seeking (shortest
arc at flip start, ties clockwise, no flip when already on the player's lane) —
matches the original's menace and is deterministic; a random walk would make
early levels aimless and is harder to reason about in tests.

## D29. fireInterval 0.15 s + anti-camping test; spikeTrim raised to 0.08 (review round 3)

Review proved hold-fire lane camping was invulnerable through level 10: auto-fire
at 0.1 s guaranteed a shot inside every 0.125 s flip-landing window, so every rim
Flipper died mid-flip with zero timing skill. fireInterval is now 0.15 s — longer
than half a flip — so the point-blank save is a timed shot again and camping loses
to rim Flippers probabilistically (and to Fuseballs outright). The constraint
`fireInterval > flipAnimTime / 2` is recorded in the tuning table, and a §13 test
asserts a scripted stationary hold-fire run dies. spikeTrim rises
0.05 → 0.08 so the slower fire rate still satisfies the descent fairness
invariant.

*Superseded in part:* D40 corrected fireInterval to 0.18 s with an
extent-aware constraint (the flipAnimTime/2 figure was insufficient), D39 added
partly-random flip targeting as the primary anti-camping mechanism, and D44 set
the current anti-camping test (both topologies, 120 s bound). The current
numbers live in §8.3 and §13; this entry records the original reasoning.

## D30. Bonus-life interval raised to 30,000 (review round 3)

At 20,000, a maximum tail wave (level 96+ budgets, Fuseballs killed at the rim,
capped clear bonus) was worth 19,650–22,650 — at least a break-even life per
wave, i.e. practical immortality. 30,000 restores the margin, and a §13 economy
test computes the maximum attainable tail-wave score and asserts it stays below
the interval, making the anti-immortality property machine-checked.

## D31. Rim-grab death is immediate; no carry-down gameplay (review round 3)

The original drags the grabbed player down the well. Here death resolves
instantly in the sim (one rule for all death causes); the renderer may show the
grab/drag as presentation. Recorded so fidelity reviews treat it as a choice.

## D32. Spikers do not count toward MaxOnWell (review round 3)

Spikers never fire, never leave, and cycle indefinitely; counting them let a
player park harmless Spikers in the concurrency budget and throttle all real
threats to a trickle (4 of 7 slots at level 17). Spawn concurrency now counts
threatening types only.

## D33. Enemy shot speed scales with level (EShot column) (review round 3)

A constant 0.5 depth/s left enemy fire toothless at every level while every
other pressure parameter scaled. EShot is now an anchor-table column
(0.40 → 1.10 depth/s); added now because the table's structure is frozen even
though its values are tunable.

## D34. GET_READY is a sim state; performance gate is work-time only (review round 3)

Two structural clarifications forced by review: (1) the post-death "get ready"
pause is deterministic gameplay timing, so it is now a first-class sim state
(PLAYING → GET_READY → PLAYING) with defined input handling (movement yes,
fire/zap no), rather than an undocumented sub-phase; level-start fade-in is
presentation only. (2) The §15 performance gate is per-frame main-thread work
time alone; dropped-frame percentage is informational because any rAF-derived
frame metric is display/VRR-dependent and would make acceptance depend on the
tester's monitor.

## D35. Enemies have fixed per-type colors; the level band colors the well/HUD (review round 4)

Review showed all five enemy types could legally render identically. Fixed
canonical colors (Flippers red, Tankers purple, Spikers/spikes green, Fuseballs
multicolor, Pulsars cyan/white) match the original's readability — the player
must identify types at depth to prioritize — while the 16-level band still
recolors the well itself.

## D36. A lane's electrification ends the instant its Pulsar dies (review round 4)

The alternative (lane stays lethal until pulse end) punishes the player for a
successful shot and is invisible-state gameplay. Kill-then-cross is a legitimate
skill move, consistent with the shot-before-lethality tick order.

## D37. Menus are keyboard-driven; pointer lock exists only in the play states (review round 4)

Mouse-driven menus would need a second (unlocked) mouse-input mode, contradicting
the pointer-lock-only model (D13). The app exits any held lock when the sim
leaves PLAYING/GET_READY/WARP; UI navigation converts the movement delta into
rate-limited discrete selector steps inside the sim, keeping menu behavior
deterministic and replayable.

## D38. Per-tick rim movement is clamped below half a lane (review round 4)

Unbounded mouse deltas could cross ≥ 2 lanes in one tick, tunneling through
symmetric rim contact, electrified lanes, and warp spikes (all sampled per tick
via the player-lane rule). A 0.45 lanes/tick clamp (~27 lanes/s — still double
keyboard speed) makes lane skipping impossible while keeping mouse control fast.

## D39. Mid-well flips are partly random (flipSeekBias); refines D28 (review round 5)

Review proved that *fully* player-seeking flips (D28) enable lane camping: every
climbing enemy funnels single-file into a stationary hold-fire player's own
lane and dies at range, so the player never has to move. Fix without losing the
original's menace: each mid-well flip seeks the player with probability
`flipSeekBias` (0.5) and otherwise steps to a random adjacent lane, so a share
of enemies reach the rim on *other* lanes and must be chased — clearing a wave
now requires rotating. The value is gated by the §13 anti-camping test. Rim-flip
chasing stays fully player-seeking (a rim enemy always pursues).

## D40. fireInterval raised to 0.18 s with an extent-aware constraint; refines D29 (review round 5)

D29's recorded rationale (fireInterval > flipAnimTime/2) was arithmetically
wrong: it ignored the §6.7 collision half-extents, which widen the rim-flip-
landing kill window by ~(enemy+shot extents)/shotSpeed + a tick, so at 0.15 s a
hold-fire stream auto-killed essentially every landing and the anti-camping test
could not honestly pass. The constraint is corrected to
fireInterval > flipAnimTime/2 + (enemyHalfExtent+shotHalfExtent)/shotSpeed +
one tick ≈ 0.162 s, and fireInterval is set to 0.18 s. The §9 descent-fairness
invariant still holds (trim rate 0.44 > descent 0.4 depth/s) and is re-verified
by its simulated §13 test.

## D41. The sim is constructed from an injected config, not direct module imports (review round 5)

To make the frozen-parameter golden replay implementable without a later
refactor, `sim/`'s entry point takes the full sim-affecting tuning surface
(§8.2 anchors, §8.3 constants, §7 scores, geometry) plus a seed as an injected
configuration. The app passes the live data modules; tests pass a frozen
snapshot. Also lets the test suite inject modified values to verify wiring
without asserting spec-canon numbers.

## D42. One menu-mouse exception: a TITLE click confirms (start); refines D37 (review round 5)

The title text promised "click to start" while D37 said the mouse plays no role
in menus — a contradiction. Resolved by a single explicit carve-out: in TITLE
only, a canvas click maps to confirm (→ LEVEL_SELECT), never requests pointer
lock, is consumed, and doubles as the AudioContext-unlock gesture. All other
menu clicks do nothing.

## D43. Multi-tick input apportionment: drain the mouse accumulator per tick, carry the remainder (review round 5)

When one rAF frame steps several sim ticks (or zero), each tick gets its own
snapshot: mouse movement accrues into a pending accumulator between frames and
is drained at most the per-tick clamp per tick with the remainder carried
forward (0-tick frames just accrue). This preserves total mouse movement, keeps
the per-tick speed cap honest regardless of refresh rate or hitches, and never
reuses a snapshot across ticks — so the golden replay's snapshot stream is
well-defined.

## D44. Topology mapping pinned; anti-camping verified on both topologies; one Spiker per lane (review round 6)

Three round-6 refinements: (1) geometry indices 0–7 are closed wells and 8–15
open, so each level's topology is deterministic (was implementer's choice).
(2) The anti-camping test — previously only closed wells — now runs on level 1
(closed, player mid-rim) and level 9 (open, player clamped at an end lane), the
single-direction-funnel worst case that fully player-seeking flips created;
without the open-well case a build could pass on closed wells yet allow
end-camping on ~half the levels. (3) At most one Spiker per lane (spawner and lane-switch exclude
occupied lanes), removing undefined multi-resident spike growth/kill semantics.

## D45. Round-7 refinements: bench resolution, Superzapper use-2, quit-to-title (review round 7)

(1) The performance bench is pinned to a fixed render resolution (1440×1080 at
DPR 2 = 2880×2160 backing store), since Canvas-2D fill cost scales with pixels
and an unpinned window size made criterion 7 non-reproducible. (2) The
Superzapper's second use destroys the enemy **nearest the rim** (deterministic,
ties by lowest lane index) instead of a random one — a panic button should hit
the most imminent threat, and it removes a sim-RNG draw. (3) A **Quit to title
(Q)** action on the pause overlay routes through the normal GAME_OVER flow so a
run can be abandoned without losing an earned high score; delivered as a
`uiQuit` snapshot input. Plus clarifications recorded directly in the spec:
bonus-life tick-order slot, pulseCycle sanity guard, state-hash contents,
high-score entry loop, half-up difficulty rounding, Math.sqrt dropped from the
deterministic-ops set, and the fractional-position player claw render.
